2026-08-30T17:04Z claude note #6012 CHECKPOINT READ 2026-08-30, at source rather than by page fetch (clone of zotero/zotero, PR head fetched as refs/pull/6012/head). VERDICT: seg/1 is NOT obsoleted; build it. State: still DRAFT, head 77e2c4b (Bogdan Abaev, 'fix stale test', authored 2026-08-28) -- the same SHA CONSTRAINTS.md read on 2026-08-29, so the PR has not moved in the interval. NOT merged: 77e2c4b is not an ancestor of origin/main (main tip bccaf46, 2026-08-27), true merge-base 3af8cea (2026-08-05), 50 commits ahead. The PR adds embeddings.js, lexical.js, bestMatch.js and ml.js and modifies fulltext.js and sdt.js, and none of it is on main. THE DECIDING FACT is not the PR's state but the API surface: sdt.js already ships on main, so structured extraction exists in the application today, yet server_localAPI.js on main carries ZERO sdt/structured references -- the only text surface is flat /fulltext, read from Zotero.Fulltext.getItemCacheFile(). Structured extraction therefore does not reach the consumer zoteus is, whether or not #6012 merges, and heuristic segmentation over flat text stays the only available path. Re-open this checkpoint if the local API gains a structured-text endpoint, not merely if #6012 merges
2026-08-31T15:12Z claude note #6012's chunker is NOT paragraph-aligned on the text we would feed it, and the reason is one regex. `_measureParagraphs` splits on /[^\n]+/g -- the unit is a LINE, not a blank-line-delimited paragraph -- and the grouping loop accumulates lines to `minSize` with no blank-line check anywhere, so blank lines carry no signal at all. Its paragraph policy therefore holds only where newlines already MEAN paragraph breaks: notes (HTML-derived) and SDT blocks, which is exactly what #6012 feeds it. On .zotero-ft-cache text, which the extractor hard-wraps and which ticket 0120 established is the only surface the local API exposes, it degrades to line accumulation that straddles paragraphs. Measured at PR head 77e2c4b (verification/probes/zotero-6012-chunker-probe.mjs, three arms over the same four single-topic paragraphs, varying only newline shape): one line per paragraph gives 4 chunks none straddling; hard-wrapped gives 6 chunks, 2 straddling two topics and several opening mid-sentence; hard-wrapped then unwrapped (collapse newlines inside a block, keep blank lines) returns to 4 chunks none straddling. CONSEQUENCE FOR seg/1: adopting #6012's geometry does not by itself buy alignment on our text -- the unwrap does, and it is one line, ahead of the segmenter. It also isolates the smallest change worth measuring, which spec/CONSTRAINTS.md's entry on the same PR does not cover: that entry owns the geometry (120/768/48) and the effective-budget caveat, not the newline question. Also noted: getCharacterMetrics is built for a consumer with no tokenizer and scales CJK at 1 char per token against 4, which is a ready-made answer to the token-safety half; and Intl.Segmenter at sentence granularity is a better and script-agnostic seam detector than glued-block-probe.py's [.!?]\n[A-Z] regex
2026-08-31T15:32Z claude note TARGET CLASS WIDENED by the author, 2026-08-31: edited collections split into entries too, not only dictionaries and encyclopedias. Ruling 1 already covers them in principle -- a collected volume is one Zotero item and many independently-authored chapters -- but seg/1 as specified in spec/DESIGN.md 2.2 is tuned to the dictionary and two of its clauses do not transfer. (i) THE DISCRIMINATING HEURISTIC IS A REGULARITY ASSUMPTION: heading candidates are accepted on the headword rhythm, median gap and MAD over candidate spacing, which works because ~1 850 dictionary entries sit at a near-constant 24 KB. A collection runs perhaps 10-40 chapters of wildly unequal length, so the MAD test that accepts headings there will misfire here -- it is not a matter of tuning the threshold, the statistic is measuring a property collections do not have. (ii) THE FALLBACK IS WRONG-SIZED: synthetic entries of ~6k tokens approximate a dictionary entry and are a fraction of a book chapter, so a collection that falls below confidence 0.5 is shredded into fragments that are neither entries nor chapters -- worse than the dictionary case the fallback was designed against, where it degrades gracefully. Signals a collection carries that a dictionary does not, and that the spec does not yet use: an author byline under the chapter title (no dictionary analogue, and a strong accept signal), a table of contents at the front to validate the cut set against rather than only scoring it, and chapter starts aligning with page boundaries -- available as form feeds in the newer extractor generation only, 4 708 of 8 590 PDF caches per ticket 0120, so a signal to exploit where present and never to require. CONSEQUENCE FOR X5, which gates scoped issue B: its rule samples 50 cut points from the 44.9 MB dictionary and hand-scores them against the printed dictionary, so a 45/50 verdict certifies the dictionary class and nothing else. If collections are in scope the experiment needs a second document class in its sample or an explicitly stated scope limit, and the >= 45/50 bar has to be read per class. NOT ACTED ON HERE: widening the target class changes spec/DESIGN.md 2.2 and X5's rule in 0025, so it is a ruling for spec/DECISIONS.md first, per the chain. One question rides with it -- ruling 1 makes the entry heading the citation locator, and a chapter's locator arguably needs its author too, since chapters are independently authored and that is the whole reason a collection is not one work
2026-08-31T15:37Z claude note PRIMARY TARGET CLASS RULED, 2026-08-31 (spec/DECISIONS.md): books and proceedings first, the dictionary a rare case. This supersedes the framing of the 15:32Z note above, which treated edited collections as a widening of a dictionary-centred spec -- the ruling inverts it instead, so the dictionary is the special case and the byline / table-of-contents / page-boundary signals that note listed are the PRIMARY signal set, not additions. Propagation of the spec edits is ticket 0502; this ticket's own exit criteria, which name the 44.9 MB dictionary as what seg/1 runs over, are repointed there. Note for whoever builds seg/1: X5's ground truth gets cheaper under the ruling, since a book's table of contents is mechanical where a printed dictionary needed a human with the physical book
2026-09-01T12:24Z SOTA review of segmentation prior art recorded at verification/SEGMENTER-FIELD-REVIEW-0028.md (two independent web-research passes, 2026-09-01). Key findings: Chapter Captor (EMNLP 2020) validates seg/1's pattern-then-accept architecture; the median/MAD spacing-regularity acceptance filter has no literature precedent under any name -- corroborates the ticket's own scope limit to the dictionary case; ICDAR 2013's TOC-alignment technique (fuzzy title match + page-number prior + forward-only monotonic search) can upgrade the TOC from a validator to an active search-space constraint for the book/proceedings primary class; ParsCit proves layout-free byline detection is established, though using it as a chapter-boundary accept-signal would be a new application. Findings on TOC alignment and byline detection are candidate DECISIONS.md rulings, not acted on here -- they extend seg/1 beyond its current dictionary-scoped exit criteria.
2026-09-01T12:27Z Minh Ha-Duong note ACCEPTANCE TEST STATED: 'All books, proceedings, dicts, handbooks, encyclopedia, and other PDF over 40 pages in my own zotero library are properly segmented, from the fulltext cache files.' Named document classes include long multi-chapter technical reports, a signed multi-author economics encyclopedia, an edited handbook, a single-author thesis, and books generally. Also: do not assume English -- the corpus is multilingual (French sources, a French thesis). This is broader than X5's 44,9 MB single-document sample and than the book/proceedings-vs-dictionary binary the 2026-08-31 ruling drew: the named encyclopedia in particular is signed, irregular-length entries (not the near-uniform headword case), which is a third structural shape neither X5's dictionary bar nor the book/chapter design directly covers. Not acted on here beyond recording it -- widening the acceptance test past X5 and the primary-target-class ruling needs its own DECISIONS.md entry. No specific document titles recorded here -- this is a public repo (correction, same session: an earlier version of this entry named them, see the 13:5x note below)
2026-09-01T12:32Z SOTA review extended (verification/SEGMENTER-FIELD-REVIEW-0028.md sections 9-10, third research pass, 2026-09-01) to address the acceptance test: French Title-Case detection is close to non-signal (French headings are sentence-case, not English Title Case) -- needs a French-aware gate, not a parameter tweak; Intl.Segmenter/UAX#29 generalizes for word/grapheme boundaries in space-delimited Latin scripts including French but not for sentence boundaries (locale-specific abbreviation lists) and is a cleanup layer under a heading detector, not a heading detector itself; TOC fuzzy-matching needs an NFKD accent-fold + case-fold step it does not yet have (standard IR practice, no design trade-off, shippable without its own ruling); FinTOC (EN/FR/ES TOC extraction shared task) is the closest multilingual precedent. The named encyclopedia named a third structural class -- irregular signed multi-author encyclopedia entries, distinct from both the uniform dictionary and the irregular-book-chapter case -- with ARTFL's French Encyclopedie as the nearest precedent. Added a mapping of the four acceptance-test document classes (signed encyclopedia, edited handbook, technical report, thesis) to specific findings, none of them exercised by X5's single 44,9 MB dictionary sample.
2026-09-01T13:23Z DECISIONS.md ruling recorded 2026-09-01: a second, PDF-layout segmentation path is adopted for the acceptance test's wider corpus (books, proceedings, dicts, handbooks, encyclopedias); seg/1 (this ticket) stays live as the fallback for sub-threshold documents and PDF-access failures, not superseded. Segmenter: pdf.js (Apache-2.0, vendored) -- PyMuPDF/fitz (AGPL-3.0-or-commercial) explicitly rejected since zoteus is MIT. Tier 1 getOutline(), tier 2 a hand-rolled font-size/position heuristic on pdf.js's own text-run data (no Python/Docling bridge, ruled out explicitly), tier 3 reuses seg/1's synthetic fallback. Trigger: Zotero's indexedPages field, not form-feed counts (blind on ~45% of cached PDFs per the extract-layer measurement). Byline detection and the exact trigger-field availability are open, named in the ruling as follow-up work. See DECISIONS.md 2026-09-01 entry and verification/SEGMENTER-FIELD-REVIEW-0028.md for the evidence.
2026-09-01T13:27Z Ruling refined 2026-09-01 (author correction): no third, PDF-side fixed-size fallback. When the PDF path's two tiers (outline, layout) come up empty or low-confidence, control falls through to seg/1 itself rather than a duplicate fixed-size mechanism in PDF-page terms -- seg/1 already owns confidence-gated synthetic entries. Also: the length trigger is a fallback chain, not a single field -- indexedPages or a literal page-break count first, a character-count estimate when neither is available, so the gate degrades to an estimate rather than never firing. DECISIONS.md entry edited in place (same PR, unmerged, same-day correction, not a rewrite of settled history).
